{"currentVersion":10.61,"cimVersion":"2.2.0","id":0,"name":"PH9224GT_Phobos2000","type":"Feature Layer","description":"<DIV STYLE=\"text-align:Left;\"><DIV><DIV><P><SPAN>In a large majority of lunar and planetary surface images, impact craters are the most abundant geological features. Therefore, it is not surprising that crater detection algorithms (CDAs) are one of the most studied subjects of image processing and analysis in lunar and planetary science. We developed an Integrated CDA, consisting of: (1) utilization of DEM (digital elevation map) -based CDA; (2) utilization of an optical-based CDA; (3) re-projection of used datasets and crater coordinates from normal to rotated view and back; (4) correction of the brightness and contrast of a used optical image; and (5) tile generation for the optical-based CDA and an assembling of results with an elimination of multiple detections, in combination with a pyramid approach down to the resolution of the available DEM image; and (6) a final integration of the results of DEM-based and optical-based CDAs, including a removal of duplicates. The proposed CDA is applied to one specific asteroid-like body, the small Martian moon Phobos. The experimental evaluation of the proposed CDA is done by a manual verification of crater-candidates and a search for uncatalogued craters. The evaluation has shown that the proposed CDA was used successfully for cataloging Phobos craters. The major result is the PH9224GT \u2013 currently the most complete global catalogue of the 9224 Phobos craters. The possible applications of the new catalogue are: (1) age estimations for any selected location; and (2) comparison/evaluation of the different chronology and production functions for Phobos.<\/SPAN><\/P><\/DIV><\/DIV><\/DIV>","geometryType":"esriGeometryPoint","sourceSpatialReference":{"wkt":"GEOGCS[\"Phobos_2015\",DATUM[\"Phobos_2015\",SPHEROID[\"Phobos_2015\",11080.0,0.0]],PRIMEM[\"Reference_Meridian\",0.0],UNIT[\"Degree\",0.0174532925199433]]","xyTolerance":5.1710992340327005E-6,"zTolerance":0.001,"mTolerance":0.001,"falseX":-400,"falseY":-400,"xyUnits":1.1258999068426238E13,"falseZ":-100000,"zUnits":10000,"falseM":-100000,"mUnits":10000},"copyrightText":"[1] G. Salamunićcar, S. Lončarić, P. Pina, L. Bandeira, J. Saraiva, 2013, Integrated method for crater detection from topography and optical images and the new PH9224GT catalogue of Phobos impact craters, Advances in Space Research, accepted. URL: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2013.11.006.","parentLayer":null,"subLayers":[],"minScale":0,"maxScale":0,"drawingInfo":{"renderer":{"type":"classBreaks","authoringInfo":{"type":"classedColor","classificationMethod":"esriClassifyManual"},"field":"Radius_km","classificationMethod":"esriClassifyManual","minValue":0.00901,"classBreakInfos":[{"symbol":{"type":"esriSMS","style":"esriSMSCircle","color":[115,223,255,255],"size":4,"angle":0,"xoffset":0,"yoffset":0,"outline":{"color":[0,0,0,255],"width":0.7}},"classMaxValue":0.035537,"label":"0.009010 - 0.035537"},{"symbol":{"type":"esriSMS","style":"esriSMSCircle","color":[188,235,176,255],"size":4,"angle":0,"xoffset":0,"yoffset":0,"outline":{"color":[0,0,0,255],"width":0.7}},"classMaxValue":0.037028,"label":"0.035538 - 0.037028"},{"symbol":{"type":"esriSMS","style":"esriSMSCircle","color":[255,255,191,255],"size":4,"angle":0,"xoffset":0,"yoffset":0,"outline":{"color":[0,0,0,255],"width":0.7}},"classMaxValue":0.063555,"label":"0.037029 - 0.063555"},{"symbol":{"type":"esriSMS","style":"esriSMSCircle","color":[245,161,122,255],"size":4,"angle":0,"xoffset":0,"yoffset":0,"outline":{"color":[0,0,0,255],"width":0.7}},"classMaxValue":0.535274,"label":"0.063556 - 0.535274"},{"symbol":{"type":"esriSMS","style":"esriSMSCircle","color":[245,0,0,255],"size":4,"angle":0,"xoffset":0,"yoffset":0,"outline":{"color":[0,0,0,255],"width":0.7}},"classMaxValue":8.923759,"label":"0.535275 - 8.923759"}]},"transparency":0,"labelingInfo":null},"defaultVisibility":true,"extent":{"xmin":-1